d/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/state.json" "file_sha256" "399b2a4cb52da66a546cfbb43cdfdb6df844253a0b0020094008416ff9dfc351"} {"question" "q-335108" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5\\sin(5t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/state.json" "file_sha256" "399b2a4cb52da66a546cfbb43cdfdb6df844253a0b0020094008416ff9dfc351"} {"question" "q-335110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices $A$ and ${e}^{At}$ above, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}).$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/state.json" "file_sha256" "399b2a4cb52da66a546cfbb43cdfdb6df844253a0b0020094008416ff9dfc351"} {"question" "q-335110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/state.json" "file_sha256" "399b2a4cb52da66a546cfbb43cdfdb6df844253a0b0020094008416ff9dfc351"} {"question" "q-335110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "301f6706f2d15d9c1bc5e5f66232fbd44dd9ad2062d3bd1edaa180642d2c4278"} {"question" "q-335110" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/state.json" "file_sha256" "399b2a4cb52da66a546cfbb43cdfdb6df844253a0b0020094008416ff9dfc351"} {"question" "q-335110" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6\\sin(6t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/state.json" "file_sha256" "399b2a4cb52da66a546cfbb43cdfdb6df844253a0b0020094008416ff9dfc351"} {"question" "q-335110" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/state.json" "file_sha256" "399b2a4cb52da66a546cfbb43cdfdb6df844253a0b0020094008416ff9dfc351"} {"question" "q-335110" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6\\cos(6t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/state.json" "file_sha256" "399b2a4cb52da66a546cfbb43cdfdb6df844253a0b0020094008416ff9dfc351"} {"question" "q-335110" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/state.json" "file_sha256" "399b2a4cb52da66a546cfbb43cdfdb6df844253a0b0020094008416ff9dfc351"} {"question" "q-335110" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6\\cos(6t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/state.json" "file_sha256" "399b2a4cb52da66a546cfbb43cdfdb6df844253a0b0020094008416ff9dfc351"} {"question" "q-335110" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/state.json" "file_sha256" "399b2a4cb52da66a546cfbb43cdfdb6df844253a0b0020094008416ff9dfc351"} {"question" "q-335110" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6\\sin(6t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/state.json" "file_sha256" "399b2a4cb52da66a546cfbb43cdfdb6df844253a0b0020094008416ff9dfc351"} {"question" "e-21271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $e^{At}$ for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/bf972d6172dc5282be366a87138ac9088216484082251482383de605c720b568.png) \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $1$ and $4.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/example-21271.json" "file_sha256" "c9fda11dadf837ee93a8e0f44fadb9f49204bf84013cd8a749ed2830d30b340f"} {"question" "e-21271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=4t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ r(4t)={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3t\\alpha_1 = e^{4t} - e^{t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_1 = \\dfrac{1}{3t}\\bigl(e^{4t}-e^{t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $4$ and subtracting the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3\\alpha_0 = 4e^{t} - e^{4t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigl(4e^{t}-e^{4t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}+4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t} \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-3(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-{e}^{4t} \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((4{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{t})+(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t})) \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/104a4830d44821dbc65ad9c832da0323b7dd9e90c934fdfc7a21c175845231d2.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/example-21271.json" "file_sha256" "c9fda11dadf837ee93a8e0f44fadb9f49204bf84013cd8a749ed2830d30b340f"} {"question" "e-21272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $e^{At}$ for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/3f97cbbd4715d8191dc5b0c410a784b556b068ed5e3e57fe0dc16ee8f52655cc.png) \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $3 \\pm 2\\textrm{i}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/example-21272.json" "file_sha256" "3e00e8ccdd55f1db1035e88b0ef7e5ad564f3eecc918aa4e7b4f0fa661867549"} {"question" "e-21272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= (3+2\\textrm{i}) t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2= (3-2\\textrm{i}) t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((3+2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(3+2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(3+2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ (3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+2{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)), \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/dec5e32e503bf178832f6cf08b51767d2f76fb308e9a9d07c4e805389507bbfd.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/example-21272.json" "file_sha256" "3e00e8ccdd55f1db1035e88b0ef7e5ad564f3eecc918aa4e7b4f0fa661867549"} {"question" "e-21273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $e^{At}$ for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/9657b1346f9aa79eaa2d05d0a94b5e13745fedc83b035cbd59f1efdb10ce19bf.png) \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $6,$ and it has multiplicity two.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/example-21273.json" "file_sha256" "503f4b5bc9d8d4728b3e23abcb28baa73d05a0d4f986e51e3309f551e43137ba"} {"question" "e-21273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 6t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(6t)={e}^{6t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(6t)={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 = e^{6t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n6t e^{6t} + \\alpha_0 = e^{6t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = e^{6t}(1-6t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =8{e}^{6t}t+{e}^{6t}(1-6t) \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(1+2t), \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-2{e}^{6t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(-2t), \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2{e}^{6t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(2t), \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4{e}^{6t}t+{e}^{6t}(1-6t) \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/70c4e625ac52b04e9a1fb258470138050a51cdacaced8714cd2a313782055c3f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/example-21273.json" "file_sha256" "503f4b5bc9d8d4728b3e23abcb28baa73d05a0d4f986e51e3309f551e43137ba"} {"question" "e-21724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/assets/f97a3d1392b000bb1f02f0f8cd567b72d1a6792e4bc9da41ea24cc4ebbc7208f.png)\nGiven the matrices $A$ and $e^{At}$ above, compute $\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t} \\big(e^{At}\\big).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/example-21724.json" "file_sha256" "a32d6845f682ff1b330d8bf71075419a2c1779afb24b17fde25085f1827b781f"} {"question" "e-21724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t} \\big(e^{At}\\big) = A \\, e^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & t{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & (t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058518/example-21724.json" "file_sha256" "a32d6845f682ff1b330d8bf71075419a2c1779afb24b17fde25085f1827b781f"}] "usage" {"q-323878" [] "e-21271" [] "e-21724" [] "e-21273" [] "e-21272" []} "basis" 1878 "retractions" [[17592186221861 :question/problem "Find $e^{At}$ for $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-4$ and $1$.*\n\n| $e^{At} = \\frac{1}{5}$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-7e^{-4t} + 7e^{t}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $-2e^{-4t} + 7e^{t}$ |  |  |\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"finding-the-matrix-exponent-for-a-matrix-with-distinct-complex-eigenvalues\"></a>"] [17592186221091 :question/problem "Find $e^{At}$ for $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $1$ and $4$.*"] [17592186221091 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{At} &= α_{1}At + α_{0}I \\\\\n&= α_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t + α_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}α_{1}t + α_{0} & 0 \\\\ -3α_{1}t & 4α_{1}t + α_{0}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of $At$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=t, \\qquad \\lambda_2=4t\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{r(t) = e^{t}; r(4t) = e^{4t}⇒{tα_{1} + α_{0} = e^{t}; 4tα_{1} + α_{0} = e^{4t}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3t\\alpha_1 = e^{4t} - e^{t} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\alpha_1 = \\dfrac{1}{3t}\\bigl(e^{4t}-e^{t}\\bigr)\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $4$ and subtracting the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3\\alpha_0 = 4e^{t} - e^{4t} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\alpha_0 = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigl(4e^{t}-e^{4t}\\bigr)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nα_{1}t + α_{0} &= (\\frac{1}{3t}(e^{4t} - e^{t}))t + \\frac{1}{3}(4e^{t} - e^{4t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(e^{4t} - e^{t} + 4e^{t} - e^{4t}) \\\\\n&= e^{t} \\\\\n- 3α_{1}t &= -3(\\frac{1}{3t}(e^{4t} - e^{t}))t \\\\\n&= e^{t} - e^{4t} \\\\\n4α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 4(\\frac{1}{3t}(e^{4t} - e^{t}))t + \\frac{1}{3}(4e^{t} - e^{4t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}((4e^{4t} - 4e^{t}) + (4e^{t} - e^{4t})) \\\\\n&= e^{4t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} & 0 \\\\ e^{t} - e^{4t} & e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186221092 :question/problem "Find $e^{At}$ for $\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $3 \\pm 2\\textrm{i}$.*"] [17592186221092 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{At} &= α_{1}At + α_{0}I \\\\\n&= α_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t + α_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}7α_{1}t + α_{0} & 4α_{1}t \\\\ -5α_{1}t & - α_{1}t + α_{0}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues or $At$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= (3+2\\textrm{i}) t, \\qquad \\lambda_2= (3-2\\textrm{i}) t\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr((3 + 2\\textrm{i})t) &= e^{(3 + 2\\textrm{i})t} \\\\\nα_{1}(3 + 2\\textrm{i})t + α_{0} &= e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n(3α_{1}t + α_{0}) + 2α_{1}\\textrm{i}t &= e^{3t}\\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}e^{3t}\\sin (2t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n{3α_{1}t + α_{0} = e^{3t}\\cos (2t); 2tα_{1} = e^{3t}\\sin (2t)⇒{α_{1} = \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t); α_{0} = e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}\\sin (2t))\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t + e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) + e^{3t}\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\\\\n&= e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) + 2\\sin (2t)), \\\\\n4α_{1}t &= 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t \\\\\n&= 2e^{3t}\\sin (2t), \\\\\n- 5α_{1}t &= -5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t), \\\\\n- α_{1}t + α_{0} &= -\\frac{1}{2t}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\cdot t + e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) + e^{3t}\\cos (2t) - \\frac{3}{2}e^{3t}\\sin (2t) \\\\\n&= e^{3t}(\\cos (2t) - 2\\sin (2t))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) + 2\\sin (2t) & 2\\sin (2t) \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}\\sin (2t) & \\cos (2t) - 2\\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186221093 :question/problem "Find $e^{At}$ for $\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $6$, and it has multiplicity two.*"] [17592186221093 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{At} &= α_{1}At + α_{0}I \\\\\n&= α_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t + α_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8α_{1}t + α_{0} & -2α_{1}t \\\\ 2α_{1}t & 4α_{1}t + α_{0}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThe only eigenvalue of $At$ is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 6t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{r(6t) = e^{6t}; \\frac{d}{dλ}r(6t) = e^{6t}⇒{6tα_{1} + α_{0} = e^{6t}; α_{1} = e^{6t}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n$\\alpha_1 = e^{6t}$.\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n$6t e^{6t} + \\alpha_0 = e^{6t} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\alpha_0 = e^{6t}(1-6t)$.\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 8e^{6t}t + e^{6t}(1 - 6t) \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(1 + 2t), \\\\\n- 2α_{1}t &= -2e^{6t}t \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(-2t), \\\\\n2α_{1}t &= 2e^{6t}t \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(2t), \\\\\n4α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 4e^{6t}t + e^{6t}(1 - 6t) \\\\\n&= e^{6t}(1 - 2t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 2t & -2t \\\\ 2t & 1 - 2t\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186221135 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven the matrices $A$ and $e^{At}$ above, compute $\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t} \\big(e^{At}\\big)$."] [17592186221135 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t} \\big(e^{At}\\big) = A \\, e^{At}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(e^{At}) &= Ae^{At} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} & te^{t} \\\\ 0 & e^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} & (t + 1)e^{t} \\\\ 0 & e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [[17592186220076 :answer-field/id] [17592186220076 :answer-field/key] [17592186220076 :answer-field/type] [17592186220077 :answer-field/id] [17592186220077 :answer-field/key] [17592186220077 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
